          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            “Thin elements” occur in various contexts. The basic idea is that many
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+category+theory"} higher categorical structures
            have cells (of
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+shapes+for+higher+structures"} various shapes
            = succeed "-like,\u201D" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence"} equivalence
            = succeed "," do
              %strong thin
            %strong thin structure
            or a
            = succeed "." do
              %strong stratification
          %h2#thin_cells_in_higher_groupoids Thin cells in higher groupoids
          %p
            In the simplicial
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/singular+complex"} singular complex
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S X
            of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo :
                  %msup
                    %mi Δ
                    %mi n
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f: \Delta^n \to X
            is called
            %strong thin
            if it factors through a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/retraction"} retraction
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi r
                  %mo :
                  %msup
                    %mi Δ
                    %mi n
                  %mo →
                  %msubsup
                    %mi Λ
                    %mi i
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo −
                      %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} r: \Delta^n \to \Lambda^{n-1}_i
            to some horn of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi Δ
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta^n
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kan+complex"} Kan
            condition on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S X
            can be strengthened to say that every horn in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S X
            has a
            %em thin
            filler. There are also, usually, other non-thin fillers which depend on
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          %p
            It is difficult to decide which laws these thin elements should satisfy. Keith Dakin’s axioms on thin elements in a simplicial set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi K
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
            with subsets
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi T
                      %mi n
                    %mo ⊆
                    %msub
                      %mi K
                      %mi n
                    %mo ,
                    %mi n
                    %mo ≥
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T_n \subseteq K_n, n \geq 1
            %em thin
            are
          %ol
            %li
              %p Degenerate elements are thin;
            %li
              %p Any horn has a unique thin filler;
            %li
              %p If all faces but one of a thin element are thin, then so is the remaining face.
          %p
            Ashley’s theorem, conjectured by Dakin, is that the category of such
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/T-complex"} complexes
            is equivalent to that of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/crossed+complexes"} crossed complexes
          %p
            As for relations with complexes having cells of different shapes, Brown and Higgins proved that cubical
            = succeed "-complexes" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            = succeed "-groupoids" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi omega
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} omega
            = succeed "-complex," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
          %h2#thin_structures_in_double_categories Thin structures in double categories
          %p
            Among other things, a
            %strong thin structure
            on a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/double+category"} double category
            can be seen as giving the information of which squares should be thought of as
            %em commuting
            rather than simply
            = succeed "." do
              %em containing a 2-cell
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            is a morphism of double categories
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Θ
                  %mo :
                  %mo □
                  %mi C
                  %mo →
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Theta: \square C \to D
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            is any category and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo □
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \square C
            is the double category of commuting squares in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
          %p
            Note that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            contains the categories
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi D
                    %mi v
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D_v
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi D
                    %mi h
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D_h
            of vertical and horizontal edges respectively, and it is often convenient to take
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            to be, say,
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi D
                      %mi v
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D_v
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Θ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Theta
            to be the identity on
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi D
                      %mi v
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D_v
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Θ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Theta
            defines a function
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Γ
                  %mo :
                  %msub
                    %mi D
                    %mi v
                  %mo →
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma: D_v \to D
            to the set of squares of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            (called a
            = succeed ")" do
              %em connection
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi γ
                  %mo :
                  %msub
                    %mi D
                    %mi v
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi D
                    %mi h
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \gamma: D_v \to D_h
            (called the
            %em holonomy
            of the connection
            = succeed ")." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Γ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Γ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma
            satisfies allow
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Θ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Theta
            to be recovered from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Γ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma
            in some cases.
          %p
            Thus, some thin structures contain a way to represent any vertical arrow as a horizontal arrow (or vice versa, depending on one’s conventions). Thin structures can equivalently be described by “foldings” and “connection pairs.” It turns out that, when they exist, they are unique up to a (suitably weak) notion of equivalence; see also
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/framed+bicategory"} framed bicategory
          %h2#simplicial_nerves_for_higher_categories Simplicial nerves for higher categories
          %p
            Following on the work mentioned above for thin elements in Kan complexes, one can consider thin elements in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nerves"} nerves
            of higher categories. In “The algebra of oriented simplices”, Street constructed the simplicial nerve for a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+omega-category"} strict omega-category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stratified+simplicial+set"} stratified simplicial set
            (a simplicial set equipped with a collection of “thin simplices” containing all degeneracies).
          %p
            Street and Roberts conjectured that the nerves of strict
            = succeed "-categories" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ω
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega
            %span.newWikiWord
              complicial horn
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/complicial+horn"} ?
            has a unique thin filler, and 1. for such a thin filler, if all faces but the one missing face in the complicial horn are thin, then so is the last one.
          %p
            Such stratified simplicial sets are called
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complicial+sets"} complicial sets
          %p
            A
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+complicial+set"} weak complicial set
            is a stratified simplicial set satisfying the above conditions except that thin fillers need not be unique. Street conjectured that these represent the nerves of
            %em weak
            = succeed "-categories," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ω
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega
            %em equivalences
            rather than the identities. But, since every complicial set is weak complicial, one should also require that the collection of thin simplices is “maximal” in some sense; Street gave one proposed characterization. Weak complicial sets have been studied extensively by Verity.
          %h2#references References
          %p
            Relevant references for thinness in the context of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+T-complexes"} simplicial T-complexes
            include:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ronnie+Brown"}> Ronnie Brown
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em An Introduction to simplicial T-complexes
                %a{:href => "http://ehres.pagesperso-orange.fr/Cahiers/brownem32.pdf"} Esquisses Math. 32 (1983) Part 1
            %li
              %p
                M.K. Dakin,
                %em Kan complexes and multiple groupoid structures
                Ph.D Thesis, University of Wales, Bangor, 1977.
                %a{:href => "http://ehres.pagesperso-orange.fr/Cahiers/dakinEM.pdf"} Esquisses Math. (1983) 32 Part 2
            %li
              %p
                N. Ashley,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Simplicial T-Complexes: a non abelian version of a theorem of Dold-Kan
                %a{:href => "http://ehres.pagesperso-orange.fr/Cahiers/AshleyEM32.pdf"} Esquisses Math. (1983) 32 Part 3
          %p Verity’s work can be found in
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                D. R. Verity, 2005, Complicial Sets , available from :
                = succeed "." do
                  %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/math.CT/0410412"} arXiv:math.CT/0410412
            %li
              %p
                D. Verity, 2006, Weak complicial sets I: basic homotopy theory , available from :
                = succeed "." do
                  %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/math/0604414"} arXiv:math/0604414
            %li
              %p D. R. Verity, 2006, Weak complicial sets. III. Enriched and internal quasi-category theory , (in preparation).
            %li
              %p D. R. Verity, 2007, Weak complicial sets. II. Nerves of complicial Gray-categories , in Categories in algebra, geometry and mathematical physics , volume 431 of Contemp. Math., 441–467, Amer. Math. Soc., Providence, RI.
